  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1245 PM MST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nEastern Idaho is enjoying a pleasant, albeit chilly, late-fall \nday as the region resides between weather systems, offering a \nbrief reprieve from cloud cover and precipitation. Clouds and \nmoisture will return tonight into Thursday as a potent upper- \nlevel low moves out of the northern Pacific and approaches the \nnorthern California and Oregon coasts. Moisture will overspread \neastern Idaho overnight and persist through Thursday.\n\nSouth-southwesterly flow aloft will generate favorable upslope \nenhancement around the Sun Valley region, prompting a Winter \nWeather Advisory for those zones. Snowfall totals of 3 to 6 \ninches appear likely there, with 6 to 10+ inches expected above \nroughly 6,500 feet. Across the remainder of eastern Idaho \u2014 \nincluding lower-elevation valleys \u2014 QPF looks considerably \nlighter at only a few hundredths of an inch. Downsloping flow \nacross the Snake Plain will suppress precipitation potential and\nkeep temperatures elevated through Thursday afternoon, delaying\nappreciable valley precipitation until late Thursday into \nFriday. Thursday daytime highs will be held to the 30s across \nthe Central Mountains and Eastern Highlands, while lower \nelevations reach the low-to-mid 40s, keeping any lower-elevation\nprecipitation on Thursday as rain or a rain/snow mix.\n\nConditions shift Thursday night into Friday as colder air \npushes into the region, transitioning snow levels to valley \nfloors and making snow the dominant precipitation type area-wide\non Friday. Daytime highs will fall into the 30s on Friday and \nremain there through the weekend and into the early part of next\nweek. Consistent with a persistent dry bias seen in recent \nevents, National Blend of Models (NBM) QPF appears too low and \nhas been nudged upward across much of the forecast area. While \nexact Friday snowfall totals remain somewhat in flux, \naccumulating snow appears likely regionwide. Based on current \nguidance, 1 to 2 inches looks reasonable for the Snake Plain and\nMagic Valley, with 3 to 6 inches forecast across the Eastern \nHighlands.\n\nThe bulk of the moisture shield will cut off early Saturday\nmorning as the main system exits. However, lingering moisture\nand unstable cyclonic flow will keep light snow showers possible\naround Island Park, along the Montana Divide, and into the\nCentral Mountains over the weekend. Daytime temperatures will\nstay cold, holding in the 30s through Sunday. The upper-level\npattern remains active throughout Thanksgiving week, though the\nfirst part of next week features no strong moisture intrusions.\nEven without a major storm system early in the week, expect\npersistent cloud cover, below-normal temperatures, and\nintermittent low-end precipitation chances. Pacific moisture\nbegins to recharge closer to the Thanksgiving holiday as a low\ndevelops across the southern Gulf of Alaska and directs another\nmoisture surge toward the Pacific Northwest. This evolution\ncould set up an active weather period around the holiday, though\nat a week out, forecasters will continue to monitor model trends\nclosely in the days ahead.",
